---
description: Company project structure and framework rules for UiPath projects
alwaysApply: true
---

Project Structure Rules
Project Naming

Project names should follow this format for business process repositories:

RPA000_DomainName_ProjectName

Example:

RPA001_Finance_FaturaKontrol

Flag as Warning if the project name does not match this structure.

Do not flag template/framework repositories as Critical only because they do not follow the business process naming format.

Flag as Critical only if the naming mismatch directly breaks deployment, package identity, Orchestrator mapping, or company inventory integration.

Framework

The project should use Rocky Framework for applicable transactional RPA processes.

Check for expected framework structure, standard entry points, transaction handling, config usage, Init / Process / End Process separation, and reusable workflow structure.

Flag as Critical if Rocky Framework is absent in a production-intended transactional process where the company standard requires it.

Flag as Warning if Rocky Framework exists but is inconsistently applied.

Folder Structure

Workflows should be organized into meaningful folders such as:

Init
Process
End Process
Framework
Common
Reusable

Rocky Framework projects may use a single Framework folder if the framework itself provides equivalent separation.

Flag as Warning if workflows are placed flat under the project root without clear grouping.

Do not flag as Critical unless folder structure directly creates maintainability, runtime, or deployment risk.

CI/CD File

The project root should contain:

.gitlab-ci.yml

The PROCESS_NAME parameter inside .gitlab-ci.yml should match the project name where applicable.

Important:

Do not assume CI/CD failure only because .gitlab-ci.yml is missing in a local snapshot.
If the company pipeline is known to work, do not classify CI/CD items as Critical without direct failure evidence.
Flag as Warning if CI/CD configuration is missing from the repository and this reduces reproducibility or onboarding clarity.
Flag as Manual Check if CI/CD may be managed outside the repository.
Flag as Critical only when there is actual evidence of broken pipeline, failed restore/build, missing required pipeline variable, or deployment failure.
Packages

Unused or unnecessary packages should be removed.

Flag as Warning when package references appear unused.

Flag as Critical only if unnecessary packages introduce confirmed security, compatibility, licensing, or deployment risk.

Dependency Integrity

Do not assume CI/CD failure only because a XAML file contains AssemblyReference or namespace imports that are not visible in project.json.

If the project is known to build and deploy successfully in the company CI/CD pipeline, do not classify this as Critical.

Instead:

Flag as Warning if missing or unclear dependencies reduce portability, clean-machine reproducibility, or onboarding clarity.
Flag as Manual Check if dependency resolution may happen through internal feeds, shared libraries, local templates, Studio cache, or pipeline-level restore logic.
Flag as Critical only if there is actual evidence of restore/build failure, missing package errors, broken pipeline logs, or runtime load errors.
